X('import-policy-free-restricted-prohibited',[
 ('Reading the policy column',['The import policy sits in the ITC(HS) schedule published by DGFT, in the column next to each eight-digit code. The entry is not always the whole story: many codes carry a policy condition, shown as a note, that adds a requirement such as a registration, a standard or an authorisation. The note is part of the policy, so read it with the code.','Policy changes by DGFT notification, sometimes with little warning, and a code that was Free last year can become Restricted. For that reason the status should be checked again at the time of ordering and again before the goods ship, not once at the start of a project.']),
 ('A worked example',['A trader plans to import a batch of personal computers. The code falls under heading 8471, and in our <a href="customs-duty-calculator" style="color:#0A6CC2">duty calculator</a> the line is marked Restricted. That tells the trader to check the current import authorisation rules before paying the supplier, because Restricted goods can be stopped at the port if the permission is missing. The same trader importing printed circuit assemblies under a different code may find the policy Free and need nothing more than the usual bill of entry.','The lesson is to check the exact eight-digit code, not the product name. Two items that look alike can sit under codes with different policy.']),
 ('Things that look Free but are not',['A Free code does not mean no duty. It only means no import licence is needed. Duty and IGST are still payable.','Other laws can apply on top: food safety, drug, wireless, BIS and plant quarantine rules are separate from the DGFT policy.','Second-hand goods, samples and gifts can follow different conditions from new commercial goods under the same code.'])],
 [('Does Free mean I pay no duty?','No. Free only means no import authorisation is needed under DGFT policy. Basic customs duty, the surcharge and IGST are charged as usual.')])
X('courier-import-into-india',[
 ('Who it suits and who it does not',['Courier clearance is built for small, urgent, high-value parcels: samples, spare parts, documents, e-commerce orders and gifts. The courier company files the declaration for you, so you do not need a customs broker for each parcel. The route has a value ceiling set by the courier regulations, and some goods are not allowed through it at all, so check the current limit and the excluded list with the courier before you ship.','For regular commercial cargo above a few hundred kilograms, air cargo or sea freight with a broker is usually cheaper and gives you more control over the paperwork.']),
 ('How the clearance works',['The courier sends the manifest and bill details to Customs before the parcel lands. Customs assesses the duty, the courier pays it, and the courier collects it from the receiver with its own charges at delivery. If the importer is a business, an IEC and a GST number are expected on the paperwork. A private person without an IEC can import goods for personal use within the rules.','Delay usually comes from a vague description on the invoice, a missing value or a missing KYC detail. The description should say what the item is and what it is made of, not just parts or goods.']),
 ('Costs to expect',['Duty and IGST on the value of the goods, as on any import. Use the calculator for a first figure.','The courier\'s own clearance and handling fee, which is usually charged per shipment.','A possible penalty or return if the goods are restricted, or if the value declared is queried and not supported by an invoice.'])],
 [('Do I need an IEC to receive a courier parcel?','For personal use of low value, usually no. For commercial goods, customs expects an importer-exporter code and a GST number. The courier will tell you what it needs for your parcel.')])
X('advance-bill-of-entry-prior-filing',[
 ('The practical timeline',['A broker prepares the bill once the commercial invoice, packing list and the draft or final bill of lading are in hand. Customs rules allow the bill to be filed before the vessel or aircraft arrives, within a limit set in the regulations, so check the current number of days with your broker. If the bill is filed in time, assessment can be done while the cargo is still at sea, and the importer pays the duty as soon as the goods are manifested.','The result is that the goods can be cleared soon after landing, instead of waiting for the filing, assessment and payment to start from zero.']),
 ('What can go wrong',['The bill of lading number or the vessel changes after filing. The bill then needs an amendment, and you may lose part of the time saved.','The invoice value or description in the filing does not match the final documents. This is the most common cause of a query.','The goods turn out to be on a later vessel. The prior bill will lapse or be re-filed under the rules, and the cost of that is yours.']),
 ('Which duty rate applies',['The rate that applies is generally the one in force on the date the import manifest is delivered, which is the arrival, and not the day you filed. If a rate change is expected, filing early does not lock in the old rate. Ask your broker about the relevant date for your cargo before you plan around a budget announcement.'])],
 [('Can I file the bill of entry before the ship arrives?','Yes, within the time limit in the regulations. Your broker can confirm the current limit and file once the shipping documents are available.')])
X('provisional-assessment-customs',[
 ('When it is used in practice',['Importers ask for provisional assessment when something about the bill cannot be settled at the time of filing: a test report that is still awaited, a price that depends on a later adjustment, a royalty or licence fee that is not fixed, or a classification question under review. Customs may allow the goods to be released on a bond and a deposit or guarantee, while the final duty is worked out later.','It is a way to keep the cargo moving, not a way to avoid duty. The final order decides the tax, and the difference is paid or refunded with the interest the law provides.']),
 ('The cost and the risk',['The bond and the security tie up money or a bank guarantee. The longer the matter stays open, the longer this goes on.','If the final assessment is higher, you pay the difference and interest. If it is lower, you apply for a refund, which is a separate process.','Records matter. The department will ask for the test report, price documents or correspondence that justified the provisional route, so keep them together.']),
 ('A short example',['An importer buys a chemical whose purity decides the duty line. The lab report takes three weeks, but the cargo is paying storage every day. With a provisional assessment, the goods are released at the higher line under a bond. When the report confirms the lower line, the importer asks for the final assessment and claims the refund of the excess.'])],
 [('Does provisional assessment stop the goods being held?','The aim is to release the goods against a bond and security while the open point is settled. Release is at the discretion of the assessing officer, so apply early and with the documents ready.')])
X('ex-bond-bill-of-entry-bonded-warehouse',[
 ('How the duty is timed',['With a bonded warehouse, the importer files a warehousing bill of entry first. The goods are stored under customs control without paying duty at that stage. When the goods are needed, an ex-bond bill of entry is filed, the duty and IGST are paid on that date, and the goods leave the warehouse. The duty is worked out under the rate in force when the ex-bond bill is presented, so a rate change in between matters.','This is useful when the importer is waiting for orders, for a final buyer, or for goods that will be re-exported without duty.']),
 ('What it costs',['Warehouse rent and handling, which are charged by the warehouse keeper.','Interest on the duty if the goods stay beyond the period allowed by the law, with extensions only on application.','Compliance work: the stock register must match the goods, and a shortage or a mix-up can lead to a duty demand.']),
 ('Typical uses',['Goods imported for a project that is not ready to receive them.','High-duty goods that may be re-exported, where paying duty up front would block cash.','Stock for sale to duty-free or export-oriented customers, subject to the rules for those sales.'])],
 [('Can bonded goods be re-exported without duty?','Yes, goods can be re-exported from a bonded warehouse under the rules, and no import duty is payable if the export is done correctly. The paperwork needs to be followed closely.')])
X('fssai-import-clearance',[
 ('Documents the officer will look for',['FSSAI import licence of the importer, valid for the product category.','The bill of entry, invoice, packing list and bill of lading or airway bill.','A certificate of origin or a health or sanitary certificate where the product or country requires one.','Labels or label images that show the details the food labelling rules require, with the importer\'s name and licence number.']),
 ('Shelf life and labelling in practice',['FSSAI sets a minimum remaining shelf life for food at the time of import, so a shipment of near-expiry food can be refused. Check the current figure before you order, and do not rely on an old number, because it has been revised in the past.','Labels that are missing in the required form can often be corrected with a sticker or re-labelling at a designated place, but this must be done with permission and adds days and cost.']),
 ('How sampling works',['The authorised officer may take a sample for testing at a notified lab. The goods wait at the port or CFS until the result is available, which is the longest part of the delay. Every day adds storage and demurrage, so factor that into your cost when you plan a first shipment of a new product.','If the sample fails, the options are limited to re-export, destruction or, in some cases, a re-test under the rules. Talk to a broker before shipping a product that has not been cleared before.'])],
 [('Do I need an FSSAI licence to import food?','Yes. A business importing food for sale needs an FSSAI import licence, plus the importer-exporter code. The licence and the product must match for the clearance to go through.')])
X('gst-lut-export-letter-of-undertaking',[
 ('Filing the LUT',['The LUT is filed online on the GST portal on a standard form, usually once for each financial year, before the first export of that year. A valid LUT from the previous year does not carry over, so renew it in April or before your first shipment. A registered person who is eligible can use it to export without paying IGST.','Keep a copy of the acknowledgement. Customs and the bank may ask for the LUT number on the shipping bill or for refund purposes.']),
 ('Choosing between LUT and paying IGST',['With an LUT, you export without paying IGST and you can claim a refund of the unused input tax credit. With the IGST route, you pay tax on the export and claim it back as a refund. Most exporters choose the LUT because the cash is not tied up. If you have a lot of credit, check with your accountant which approach fits your cash flow.']),
 ('Common errors',['Exporting before the LUT is accepted or after it has lapsed. The IGST then becomes payable.','Not mentioning the LUT or the GST details on the shipping bill, which can delay the refund.','Failing to bring the export proceeds in time, since the conditions of the LUT link to export realisation.'])],
 [('Do I need a new LUT every year?','Yes. An LUT is valid for a financial year, and you should file a fresh one for the new year before you export.')])
X('duty-drawback-explained',[
 ('A simple example',['An exporter of cotton garments uses imported trims and domestic fabric. The government publishes an all industry rate for garments, a percentage of the FOB value, so the exporter does not need to prove the exact duty paid on each input. After the shipment is cleared and the shipping bill details are verified, the drawback is credited to the exporter\'s bank account.','If the exporter\'s actual duty content is much higher than the standard rate, a brand rate claim may be possible, but it needs more paperwork and a separate application.']),
 ('What the claim needs',['A shipping bill with the drawback scheme declared, the correct description and the right schedule item.','The export general manifest and the proof that the goods left India.','Bank details linked to the IEC. A mismatch here is a frequent cause of delay.']),
 ('Mistakes that cost money',['Claiming drawback and RODTEP on the same item when the rules do not allow it. Check which scheme applies to your product.','Wrong schedule code, so the claim is rejected or paid at a lower rate.','Not following up when the shipping bill shows an error. Drawback does not fix itself after the shipment has sailed.'])],
 [('Is drawback the same as RODTEP?','No. Drawback returns customs and central excise duty on inputs, while RODTEP refunds embedded taxes and duties that are not otherwise refunded. They are separate schemes with separate rates.')])
X('rodtep-explained',[
 ('How the claim is made',['The scheme rate for each product is notified against its HS code, as a percentage of the FOB value. The exporter must declare the RODTEP claim on the shipping bill, and after the export is complete, the benefit is credited as an electronic scrip that can be used to pay certain duties or sold. The rules are issued by DGFT and customs, so rates can change.','Where the rate has a value cap per unit, the benefit is the lower of the percentage and the cap.']),
 ('Details that trip people up',['The code on the shipping bill must match the notified scheme code. A near match is not enough.','The benefit is available only for exports under the conditions of the scheme, so goods exported under a scheme such as advance authorisation may be excluded.','Claims lapse. File within the time allowed, and check that the shipping bill shows the benefit before the vessel leaves.']),
 ('Planning around it',['Treat RODTEP as part of the margin, but not as something certain. Rates are reviewed, so quote an export price that works even if the rate falls. Ask your broker to include the RODTEP check in the shipping bill filing, so it is declared properly each time.'])],
 [('How do I receive the RODTEP benefit?','It is credited as an electronic scrip in the exporter\'s account after the export is processed. The scrip can be used to pay certain customs duties or transferred under the rules.')])
X('advance-authorisation-and-epcg',[
 ('Choosing between them',['Advance Authorisation is for raw materials and components that go into an exported product. You import the inputs duty free, make the product and export it, within the validity period. EPCG is for capital goods: machinery imported at a low or zero duty on the promise to export a multiple of the duty saved over several years.','The two are not interchangeable. A manufacturer with both a raw material and a machinery need can hold both, but each has its own obligation and its own records.']),
 ('Obligations to keep',['Export the products within the period the authorisation sets, with the quantity and value fixed in it.','Keep records of input use, because the authority can ask you to show that the duty-free inputs went into exported goods.','File the export obligation discharge details on time. Missing the deadline can lead to duty and interest becoming payable.']),
 ('When it goes wrong',['The most common failure is an export shortfall: the plant does not export as much as promised. In that case the duty saved, with interest, may have to be paid back. A realistic plan matters more than a large one.','Another is a mismatch between the import item and the product declared in the authorisation. Check the code and description carefully before you apply.'])],
 [('Can a merchant exporter use Advance Authorisation?','Yes, in some cases, with the supplying manufacturer named in the authorisation. The conditions are in the Foreign Trade Policy and the handbook, so check them before you apply.')])
X('rcmc-registration-export-promotion-council',[
 ('When you will need it',['You will be asked for an RCMC when you claim certain export benefits, apply for an authorisation, or register with a promotion council to join a trade fair or delegation. It also shows that you are a bona fide exporter in your sector.','Not every exporter needs one on the first shipment, but most who grow find they need it sooner or later.']),
 ('The application',['Choose the council that matches your product, such as engineering, apparel, chemicals or agriculture products. Apply online with your IEC, GST registration, PAN, bank details and a proof of business.','The council issues the certificate for a set period and charges a fee based on the category of membership. Keep the certificate details on file, because they are quoted in later applications.']),
 ('Keeping it in force',['Renew before the expiry date. A lapsed RCMC can hold up a scheme claim.','Update the council if your address, product range or constitution changes.','Check that the product codes on the certificate cover the goods you now export.'])],
 [('Is RCMC the same as an IEC?','No. The IEC is the importer-exporter code issued by DGFT, and it is needed to import or export. The RCMC is a membership certificate from an export promotion council, used for benefits and schemes.')])
X('certificate-of-origin-guide',[
 ('Preferential and non-preferential in practice',['A non-preferential certificate states where the goods were made and is often needed for customs clearance or a letter of credit. A preferential certificate goes further: it allows the buyer to claim a reduced or zero duty under a trade agreement such as India\'s agreements with the UAE, ASEAN or Japan.','The preferential certificate has a specific format for each agreement, and it follows the rules of origin of that agreement. A normal chamber certificate will not be accepted for a preference claim.']),
 ('Before you apply',['Work out how the product qualifies: wholly obtained, a value addition threshold or a change in tariff heading. Keep the costing and the supplier invoices that prove it.','Check that the HS code and description on the certificate match the invoice and the shipping bill.','Apply before the goods ship if you can. Some issuing bodies will not issue a certificate long after shipment, or charge extra.']),
 ('Common mistakes',['Declaring Indian origin on goods that are only packed or re-labelled in India.','Differences in spelling, quantity or invoice number between the certificate and the other documents.','Missing the validity period or the time limit for presenting the certificate at destination.'])],
 [('Who issues a certificate of origin in India?','Non-preferential certificates are issued by chambers of commerce and authorised bodies, and preferential ones by the agencies the agreement names. The exporter applies online with the invoice and supporting documents.')])
X('export-documents-checklist',[
 ('A way to check before the cargo moves',['Have the buyer\'s purchase order or contract and a signed commercial invoice. These are the base of every other document.','Match the invoice with the packing list, in quantities, weights and descriptions. A mismatch is the first thing a bank or customs officer notices.','Confirm the HS code and any export policy condition for the product.','Collect the permits the goods need: a fumigation certificate, a quality or phytosanitary certificate, or a licence.','Arrange the transport document: a bill of lading for sea or an airway bill for air.']),
 ('After the shipment',['Collect the shipping bill and the proof of export, and keep copies of every document, because the bank and the tax authorities can ask for them for several years. File for the benefits you claim, such as drawback or RODTEP, and the GST refund if applicable. Track the buyer\'s payment against the invoice and plan for the bank\'s realisation certificate.']),
 ('Where exporters lose time',['Last minute corrections to the invoice after the shipping bill is filed.','A late certificate of origin or inspection report that misses the vessel cut-off.','A different consignee name on the invoice and the bill of lading.'])],
 [('How long should I keep export documents?','Keep them for as long as the law and your bank require, which is usually several years. Ask your accountant for the period that applies to you.')])
X('merchant-exporter-vs-manufacturer-exporter',[
 ('How the paperwork differs',['A manufacturer exporter shows the factory address and its own production records, and can claim benefits linked to manufacturing. A merchant exporter buys goods from a supplier and exports them, so the shipping bill names the supplier\'s details and the invoice trail must connect the two.','Both need an IEC and a GST registration. The merchant exporter has more to document, because it must prove where the goods came from.']),
 ('GST and the supplier',['A merchant exporter can buy from the supplier at a lower GST rate in some cases, under a specified procedure, if the goods are exported within the time limit. This is a rule with conditions, and a missed condition can bring the tax back. Check with your accountant before you use it.','Either type of exporter can export under an LUT and claim refunds of unused credit.']),
 ('Choosing the model',['If you own a factory or have a contract manufacturer, the manufacturer route gives more control. If you are building a trading business with several suppliers, the merchant route is faster to start. Many exporters begin as merchants and add manufacturing later.'])],
 [('Can a trader export without owning a factory?','Yes. A merchant exporter buys goods from manufacturers and exports them, with an IEC and a GST registration.')])
X('export-proceeds-realisation-fema-ebrc',[
 ('The time limit and what counts',['Under FEMA and the RBI directions, export proceeds must be received in India and realised within the period the rules set, counted from the date of export. The period and the exceptions are set by RBI, so check the current rule with your bank. Payment can be in a permitted foreign currency or in rupees under the rules.','Realisation is recorded by the bank against the shipping bill. If payment arrives in parts, each part is matched to the invoice.']),
 ('What to keep',['The bank realisation certificate, called the e-BRC, which the DGFT system uses to support scheme claims.','The FIRC or the bank advice that shows the foreign remittance.','Correspondence with the buyer about any delay, short payment or discount.']),
 ('When a buyer does not pay',['Act early. Ask the bank about an extension before the limit ends, and keep proof of your recovery efforts. Credit insurance and a letter of credit reduce this risk from the start. For a larger unpaid amount, ask your bank or a lawyer about the options, including the buyer\'s country remedies.'])],
 [('What is an e-BRC?','It is the electronic bank realisation certificate that confirms the bank has received the export proceeds against a shipping bill. DGFT uses it to verify the export for benefits.')])
